From 35b15c7a340878c437fd009c07c90fd98fe552e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Besard <tim.besard@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 15:29:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Remove the SIGFPE handler

On x86-64, device initialization installed a process-wide SIGFPE handler that
skipped integer divisions by zero in the pthread driver's worker threads, and
otherwise chained to the previous handler. This replaced the host
application's handler, and the chaining called the saved action unconditionally,
so with the default disposition (SIG_DFL, a null pointer) a division by zero in
the host application raised SIGSEGV instead of SIGFPE. It also didn't cover the
basic driver, which runs kernels on the application's thread, and disabled the
SanitizeUBofDivRem pass, so an integer division by zero in a kernel crashed
there.

Always use the SanitizeUBofDivRem pass on x86 instead, as OpenMP and TBB builds
already did, and remove the ENABLE_SIGFPE_HANDLER option. Include a marker in
the CPU device build hash, so program binaries built without the pass are
rejected instead of trapping.

Extend test_signal_handlers to check SIGFPE and to launch a kernel dividing by
zero, also with the basic driver.

(cherry picked from commit f17b6af2bb4ac9cd280c924170ef3499bb43af46)
